tyle>@media screen and (min-width: 768px){.stk-09d8147 {flex:var(--stk-flex-grow, 1) 1 calc(70% - var(--stk-column-gap, 0px) * 1 \/ 2 ) !important;}}<\/style><div class=\"stk-column-wrapper stk-block-column__content stk-container stk-09d8147-container stk--no-background stk--no-padding\"><div class=\"stk-block-content stk-inner-blocks stk-09d8147-inner-blocks\">\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading has-teal-color has-text-color\">Emma Rawlings Smith CGeog, FRGS, FHEA, FCIEA<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-green-color has-text-color wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Expert in sustainability and geography education<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Emma Rawlings Smith is Lead Researcher at AQA, where she leads research on educational assessment. She previously served as Lecturer in Sustainability and Geography Education at the University of Southampton. Her research interests span 11\u201318 geography education, teacher education, place-based learning, and sustainability. Emma began her career teaching secondary Geography and Environmental Science, before moving into higher education, where she has spent the past decade as a geography teacher educator.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">She is a Fellow of the Royal Geographical Society and serves as a Chartered Geographer assessor. Emma is also co-convenor of the British Educational Research Association\u2019s Curriculum, Pedagogy and Assessment (CAP) Special Interest Group, a member of the International Teacher Education Research Collective (ITERC), and a member of the Association of Educational Assessment\u2019s Holistic Education Steering Group.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Sustainability underpins much of Emma\u2019s work, and has shaped her contributions across research, teaching, and professional communities, and informs her commitment to advancing high-quality climate change education.<\/p>\n<\/div><\/div><\/div>\n<\/div><\/div>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Our par